    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d288">

                <pb facs="1072" n="1033" sameAs="frus:frus1977-80v13/pg1033"/>

                <head>288. Memorandum From the President’s Assistant for National Security Affairs
                        (Brzezinski) to President
                        Carter<anchor n="1" xml:id="d288-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1980-01-04">January 4, 1980</date>
                    </dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>
                        Harold Brown’s China Trip</item>
                </list>
                <p>
                    Harold Brown has sent in a message
                    requesting further guidance on a few items.</p>
                <p>—Do we plan to follow up on Harold’s suggestion of trying to arrange a trip to
                    Beijing by the Pakistani Defense Minister during Harold’s stay there? He assumes
                    we will not and agrees with that but wants to know for sure.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d288-f2"/>
                </p>
                <pb facs="1073" n="1034" xml:id="pg1034"/>
                <p>—Harold points out that the Chinese will be extremely interested in what support
                        [<hi rendition="#italic">1 line not declassified</hi>] we are prepared to
                    provide the Afghan insurgents. [<hi rendition="#italic">3½ lines not
                        declassified</hi>]<anchor n="3" xml:id="d288-f3"/>
                </p>
                <p>—Harold suggests we brief selected Congressional Leaders on some of the key
                    aspects of his visit and particularly the COCOM differential. He also suggests that we mention the OTH radar to Senator Glenn in “absolute confidence.” If you approve, we will
                    coordinate an appropriate statement and approach to selected Congressional
                        Leaders.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d288-f4"/>
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d288-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d288-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Carter Library, National Security Affairs, Brzezinski Material, Agency File, Box 6, Defense Department:
                    1/80. Top Secret; Sensitive. Sent for action.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d288-f2" n="2" xml:id="d288-f2-content">
                <p>Carter checked the option “Do not follow up” and initialed “J.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d288-f3" n="3" xml:id="d288-f3-content">
                <p>Carter checked the option “Approve; inform the Chinese” and initialed “J.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d288-f4" n="4" xml:id="d288-f4-content">
                <p>Carter checked the Approve option and initialed “J.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
